[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst did not provide the requested physical exam findings — likely because the test requires real-time clinical observation, not database retrieval. Since “Inspect hands…” is a clinician-performed examination (not a lab/imaging test), and the Analyst cannot perform it, I must instead request an *objective, measurable test* that can be retrieved — per the system’s design where the Analyst returns results for specific lab/imaging studies. I need to pivot to domain #8: most informative laboratory/imaging tests. Given the subacute systemic symptoms (fatigue, dyspnea, dizziness, cognitive slowing, fingertip pain), top diagnostic priorities are:  
- Complete blood count (CBC) to assess for anemia, thrombocytosis/thrombocytopenia, or leukoerythroblastic changes;  
- Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) to evaluate electrolytes, renal/hepatic function, calcium, glucose;  
- Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — despite patient denial of thyroid disease, subclinical hypothyroidism is common and fits the symptom cluster;  
- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP) — to screen for inflammation (e.g., vasculitis, autoimmune disease);  
- Vitamin B12 and folate — given neuropathic/cognitive symptoms and smoking (risk factor for deficiency).  
Among these, CBC is the single most sensitive initial test for anemia (which would explain fatigue, dyspnea, dizziness, cognitive slowing) and is universally available. It also may show microcytosis (suggesting iron deficiency or thalassemia) or macrocytosis (B12/folate deficiency, alcohol, drugs). So CBC is the highest-yield first test.

[TEST] Complete blood count (CBC) with differential and peripheral blood smear review.

